---
import {Image} from "astro:assets";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Button from "../../components/Button.astro";
import HomePageLink from "../../components/HomePageLink.astro";
import ContactSection from "../../components/ContactSection.astro";
import RevealScript from "../../components/RevealScript.astro";
import speakingPhoto from "../../assets/about-speaking.png";
import {site} from "@/lib/site";
---

<Layout title="About — Becca Bailey" description={site.aboutDescription}>
  <div>
    <h1 class="mb-6 md:mb-8">About</h1>

    <section
      class="reveal mb-12 grid gap-8 md:mb-16 md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_min(380px,38%)] md:items-start md:gap-10 lg:gap-12"
    >
      <div class="max-w-prose editorial-prose space-y-4 text-text-muted">
        <p class="text-text">
          I'm a software engineer, writer, and researcher. I write to help
          people in tech feel seen.
        </p>
        <p>
          I've spent my career moving between disciplines that don't always look
          related on paper. I started as a music teacher. I became a software
          engineer, then an engineering manager and technical leader. I've built
          developer tools, design systems, accessible interfaces, and data
          visualization products; led engineers and technical initiatives; and
          spoken at software conferences.
        </p>
        <p class="text-text">The common thread has always been people.</p>
        <p>
          I'm interested in why systems produce the outcomes they do—and what
          happens when they don't work for the people inside them.
        </p>
        <p>
          In software, that has meant thinking beyond whether something
          technically works. Who can use it? Can they understand it? What
          organizational structures help engineers do good work? How do we build
          teams and technical systems that can adapt as their needs change?
        </p>
        <p>
          Increasingly, those questions have taken me outside software itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          Why does one person's work become recognition and opportunity while
          someone else's disappears? Why do organizations repeatedly reward
          behaviors that make their teams less effective? Why do workers
          experience systemic problems as personal failures? What happens to all
          of this as AI changes both the technology we build and the
          organizations building it?
        </p>
        <p>Those are questions you can't answer with code alone.</p>
        <p class="text-text">So I started writing about them.</p>
      </div>

      <figure
        class="m-0 w-full max-w-[380px] justify-self-center md:justify-self-end"
      >
        <Image
          src={speakingPhoto}
          alt="Becca Bailey speaking into a microphone on stage"
          class="image-outline aspect-[3/4] w-full rounded-md object-cover"
          width={900}
          height={1200}
          loading="eager"
        />
      </figure>
    </section>

    <div class="max-w-prose">
      <section class="reveal mb-12">
        <h2 class="mb-6">Writing &amp; research</h2>
        <div class="editorial-prose space-y-4 text-text-muted">
          <p>
            I use data, academic research, history, reporting, and my own
            experience inside the technology industry to investigate the strange
            things that happen at work—the things that make you wonder, <em
              >Wait. Is anyone else seeing this?</em
            >
          </p>
          <p>
            My projects include <a
              href="/making-things/language-of-work"
              class="underline hover:text-text">The Language of Work</a
            >, an investigation of the systems and language surrounding
            employment, and Did You See That?, where I start with something
            strange from technology, work, or culture and ask other people to
            help me figure out what we're looking at.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col items-start gap-4">
          <Button href="/writing">Read my writing</Button>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="reveal mb-12">
        <h2 class="mb-6">Engineering &amp; technical leadership</h2>
        <div class="editorial-prose space-y-4 text-text-muted">
          <p>
            I've spent more than a decade building software and leading the
            people who make it.
          </p>
          <p>
            My technical work has focused particularly on frontend architecture,
            developer experience, accessibility, design systems, and data
            visualization. I've worked as an individual contributor, engineering
            manager, consultant, technical lead, and founding CTO.
          </p>
          <p>
            I'm particularly interested in technical work where the hard
            problems aren't purely technical: building strong engineering
            organizations, making complex products understandable, improving the
            systems around how software gets built, and translating between
            engineering and the humans on either side of it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col items-start gap-2">
          <HomePageLink href="/speaking-and-writing"
            >See my technical work</HomePageLink
          >
          <HomePageLink href="/resume">Résumé</HomePageLink>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
  <ContactSection class="mt-12 md:mt-16" />
  <RevealScript />
</Layout>
